---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Splatter from '../components/Splatter.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="Deconstructing Academia is founder-led by Mabbit. Here's the mission and the method."
>
  <section class="wrap about-hero" data-palette="electric">
    <span class="about-hero__splat" aria-hidden="true"><Splatter variant="burst" /></span>
    <div class="about-hero__copy">
      <p class="about-hero__eyebrow">Inside academia · taking notes</p>
      <h1><span>Not here to make</span><strong>the gate prettier.</strong></h1>
      <p class="about-hero__deck">
        Deconstructing Academia is a public argument about knowledge: who gets to create it, whose
        language is treated as intelligent, whose experience counts as evidence, whose work gets
        cited, and who gets shut out before the conversation begins.
      </p>
      <p class="about-hero__thesis">The point is not to make academia friendlier. The point is to make knowledge harder to hoard.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="about-hero__portrait" aria-hidden="true">
      <img src="/images/characters/Mabbit-side-front-view-arms-crossed.png" alt="" width="1376" height="768" />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="method-band" data-palette="public-record">
    <div class="wrap method-grid">
      <header>
        <p class="section-label">The method</p>
        <h2>Take the second look.</h2>
        <p>Every piece here runs the same basic questions, sometimes called <em>The Second Look</em>:</p>
      </header>
      <ol>
        <li><span>01</span>What am I being shown as normal, natural, neutral, or inevitable?</li>
        <li><span>02</span>Who created the category, definition, statistic, or official story?</li>
        <li><span>03</span>What history had to disappear for this explanation to make sense?</li>
        <li><span>04</span>Whose experience, knowledge, labor, or suffering is missing?</li>
        <li><span>05</span>Who gains power, legitimacy, money, safety, or comfort when this story is accepted?</li>
        <li><span>06</span>What does reality look like when the missing information is restored?</li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap profile" data-palette="red-pen">
    <aside class="profile__rail">
      <p class="section-label">Who's asking</p>
      <h2>Mabbit Rountree</h2>
      <p class="profile__note">My favorite color is glitter. Don't bullshit me—it's a color.</p>
      <div class="profile__whonah" aria-hidden="true">
        <img src="/images/characters/Whonah-sitting-forward.png" alt="" width="256" height="470" />
      </div>
    </aside>
    <div class="profile__story">
      <p>I live in Eugene, Oregon, with my husband, Chace, and three German Shepherds: mom, dad, and daughter. A complete canine family unit, because apparently one German Shepherd was not enough. We needed a dynasty.</p>
      <p>Chace and I met on Xbox Live playing <em>Call of Duty</em> about fifteen years ago, which is either a modern love story or proof that something worthwhile can occasionally emerge from a multiplayer lobby. We've been together for most of fourteen years. On Valentine's Day 2024, we got married both legally in real life and inside the world of <em>Fallout</em>. The paperwork was real. So was the wasteland wedding.</p>
      <p>I'm also the mother of two grown daughters. Robyn is twenty-one and living a life with enough plot twists to keep things interesting. Maeve is eighteen, newly graduated from high school, and living her best life with her longtime partner, Carter. They are smart, funny, complicated, entirely themselves, and two of the best people I know.</p>
      <p>The rest of my character sheet includes raccoons, unicorns, fantasy books, LitRPG, <em>Borderlands</em>, <em>Fallout</em>, hiking, colorful gel pens, and doing most of my best work at hours when respectable people are asleep.</p>
      <blockquote>I did not arrive at academia through the tidy, institutionally approved route.</blockquote>
      <p>Before becoming a full-time student, I worked in warehouses, retail, gig delivery, and e-commerce. I raised children. I built things, abandoned things, started over, and learned how much knowledge never appears on an official transcript. Now I'm a senior at the University of Oregon studying anthropology with a minor in nonprofit administration. I'm also a freelance researcher whose work has taken me through public records, regulations, correctional facilities, procurement systems, property research, nonprofits, and statistics that looked impressive until somebody asked what the denominator was.</p>
      <p>In other words, I came to higher education with a life already in progress.</p>
      <p>Once I got here, I discovered that academia runs on two sets of rules: the ones written in the handbook and the ones everybody expects you to know without being told. Office hours. Citation hierarchies. Participation grades. Faculty etiquette. Bureaucratic vocabulary. The thousand tiny customs that get treated as common sense even though they are only common if someone taught them to you.</p>
      <blockquote>Deconstructing Academia is what happened when I got tired of pretending that was normal.</blockquote>
      <p>I built this project to take academic systems apart, trace the evidence, translate the hidden rules, and ask who benefits when those rules stay hidden. Not because expertise is fake. Not because education is pointless. Because knowledge matters too much to leave it wrapped in institutional fog.</p>
      <p>I'm not standing outside academia throwing rocks at it. I'm inside, taking notes.</p>
      <p class="profile__receipts">And I brought receipts.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="values-band">
    <div class="wrap values-grid">
      <article data-palette="field-notes">
        <p class="section-label">Editorial values</p>
        <h2>How the argument sounds</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Plainspoken. Skeptical. Funny when the absurdity earns it. Rigorous. Politically awake.</li>
          <li>A citation supports the point—it doesn't become the personality.</li>
          <li>No manufactured “both sides” paragraph when the evidence supports a conclusion.</li>
          <li>Necessary nuance stays. Cowardly hedging goes.</li>
        </ul>
      </article>
      <article data-palette="archive">
        <p class="section-label">Evidence commitments</p>
        <h2>Receipts are structural.</h2>
        <p>Deconstructing Academia criticizes institutions. That makes sloppy evidence less acceptable, not more.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap commitments" data-palette="archive">
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Claims should have receipts.</strong><span>Factual claims should trace to an identifiable source. Articles will link to or name the evidence they rely on whenever reasonably possible.</span></li>
      <li><strong>AI is not a source.</strong><span>AI tools may help locate leads, organize material, or test questions. They do not become evidence because they produced a convincing paragraph. A claim still has to survive contact with the actual source.</span></li>
      <li><strong>Statistics need context.</strong><span>Numbers should include enough information to understand what was measured, when, where, by whom, and out of how many. If the denominator or definition cannot be established, I will not pretend the number is more precise than it is.</span></li>
      <li><strong>Evidence and interpretation are different things.</strong><span>I will distinguish what a source found from what I think it means. Strong evidence can support an argument. It does not magically erase the fact that an argument is being made.</span></li>
      <li><strong>Source quality matters.</strong><span>I prefer primary records, original research, official documents, and authoritative sources when they fit the claim. Secondary and advocacy sources can still be useful, but their methods, incentives, and limitations matter.</span></li>
      <li><strong>Disagreement stays visible.</strong><span>When credible sources conflict, I will examine dates, definitions, methods, and changing conditions instead of choosing whichever version is most convenient.</span></li>
      <li><strong>Corrections stay visible too.</strong><span>Material factual corrections will be noted on the affected page with the date and what changed. Typos and formatting fixes may be corrected without ceremony. If you find something that needs another look, <a href="/contact/">tell me</a>.</span></li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .about-hero { position: relative; isolation: isolate; overflow: hidden; display: grid; grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1.35fr) minmax(15rem, 0.65fr); min-height: 39rem; align-items: end; padding-block: var(--space-5) 0; }
  .about-hero__splat { position: absolute; z-index: -1; left: -10rem; top: -10rem; width: 34rem; color: var(--accent-1); opacity: 0.2; }
  .about-hero__copy { align-self: center; padding-bottom: var(--space-5); }
  .about-hero__eyebrow,.section-label { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: var(--step--1); font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: 0.09em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--accent-1); }
  .about-hero h1 { display: grid; justify-items: start; gap: 0.12em; margin-block: var(--space-2); font-size: var(--step-5); }
  .about-hero h1 span,.about-hero h1 strong { padding: 0.05em 0.14em; color: var(--ink); font-weight: 400; line-height: 0.94; box-shadow: 7px 7px 0 rgb(0 0 0 / 48%); }
  .about-hero h1 span { background: var(--paper); transform: rotate(-0.8deg); }
  .about-hero h1 strong { background: var(--accent-1); transform: rotate(0.8deg); }
  .about-hero__deck { font-family: var(--font-typewriter); font-size: var(--step-1); }
  .about-hero__thesis { width: min(100%, 36rem); margin: var(--space-3) 0 0 8%; padding: var(--space-2); background: var(--accent-3); color: var(--ink); font-family: var(--font-annotation); font-size: var(--step-1); line-height: 1.15; transform: rotate(-1.5deg); box-shadow: 5px 5px 0 rgb(0 0 0 / 45%); }
  .about-hero__portrait { position: relative; min-height: 38rem; overflow: hidden; }
  .about-hero__portrait img { position: absolute; left: -20rem; bottom: 0; width: auto; max-width: none; height: 37rem; filter: drop-shadow(8px 7px 0 rgb(0 0 0 / 55%)); }
  .method-band { padding-block: var(--space-5); background: var(--paper); color: var(--ink); }
  .method-grid { display: grid; grid-template-columns: minmax(15rem, 0.6fr) minmax(0, 1.4fr); gap: clamp(2rem, 7vw, 6rem); }
  .method-grid header { position: sticky; top: var(--space-3); align-self: start; }
  .method-grid ol { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr); gap: 2px; margin: 0; padding: 0; list-style: none; }
  .method-grid li { min-height: 10rem; padding: var(--space-2); border: 2px solid var(--ink); font-family: var(--font-label); font-weight: 700; line-height: 1.25; }
  .method-grid li span { display: block; margin-bottom: var(--space-2); font-family: var(--font-mono); color: var(--accent-1); }
  .profile { display: grid; grid-template-columns: minmax(14rem, 0.55fr) minmax(0, 1.45fr); gap: clamp(2rem, 7vw, 6rem); padding-block: var(--space-6); }
  .profile__rail { position: sticky; top: var(--space-3); align-self: start; overflow: hidden; padding: var(--space-3) var(--space-3) 0; border: 2px solid var(--paper); border-top: 8px solid var(--accent-1); min-height: 34rem; }
  .profile__note { padding: var(--space-2); background: var(--accent-3); color: var(--ink); font-family: var(--font-annotation); font-size: var(--step-1); line-height: 1.15; transform: rotate(-2deg); }
  .profile__whonah { height: 17rem; text-align: center; }
  .profile__whonah img { height: 18rem; width: auto; margin: 0 auto; }
  .profile__story { max-width: var(--content-max); }
  .profile__story blockquote { margin: var(--space-4) 0; padding: var(--space-2); border-left: 7px solid var(--accent-1); font-family: var(--font-headline); font-size: var(--step-2); text-transform: uppercase; line-height: 1.05; }
  .profile__receipts { font-family: var(--font-headline); font-size: var(--step-3); text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--accent-1); }
  .values-band { padding-block: var(--space-5); background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--ink) 91%, white); border-block: 1px solid var(--color-border); }
  .values-grid { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1.25fr 0.75fr; gap: var(--space-3); }
  .values-grid article { padding: var(--space-3); border-top: 8px solid var(--accent-1); background: var(--ink); }
  .values-grid li { margin-bottom: var(--space-1); }
  .commitments { padding-block: var(--space-5) var(--space-6); }
  .commitments ul { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr); gap: var(--space-2); margin: 0; padding: 0; list-style: none; }
  .commitments li { display: grid; gap: var(--space-1); padding: var(--space-3); border: 2px solid var(--color-border); border-left: 6px solid var(--accent-1); }
  .commitments strong { font-family: var(--font-label); font-size: var(--step-1); }
  @media (max-width: 48rem) {
    .about-hero { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) 13rem; }
    .about-hero__portrait img { left: -21rem; }
    .method-grid,.profile { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
    .method-grid header,.profile__rail { position: static; }
    .profile__rail { min-height: 0; }
    .profile__whonah { height: 13rem; }
    .profile__whonah img { height: 14rem; }
  }
  @media (max-width: 40rem) {
    .about-hero { grid-template-columns: 1fr; min-height: 0; }
    .about-hero__copy { padding-bottom: var(--space-2); }
    .about-hero h1 { font-size: 3rem; }
    .about-hero__thesis { margin-left: 0; }
    .about-hero__thesis { width: 100%; padding-inline: 0.8rem; transform: none; }
    .about-hero__portrait { min-height: 28rem; }
    .about-hero__portrait img { left: -12.5rem; height: 27rem; }
    .method-grid ol,.values-grid,.commitments ul { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
    .method-grid li { min-height: auto; }
  }
</style>
